.TH SH-THERMO 1
.SH NAME
thermo \- shell script interface to thermo(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load thermo

.B id := ${thermo new}
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B gas air
.RB "| gas " "name mw gamma"
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B state
.RB ( pt | pv | vt )
.I a b
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.RB ( isothermal | isentropic )
.I p2
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.RB ( isobaric | isochoric )
.I t2
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B polytropic
.I p2 n
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B carnot
.I tc th p1 p2
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B otto
.I ratio t1 p1 qin
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B diesel
.I ratio cutoff t1 p1
.br
.B thermo
.I id
.B brayton
.I ratio t1 p1 t3
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.BR r | cp | cv | gamma | p | v | t | u | h | s
.B }
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.BR work | heat | du | ds
.B }
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.BR efficiency | wnet | qin | qout | states | names
.B }
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.B path
.RI [ n ]
.B }
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.B lmtd
.RB ( parallel | counter )
.I thi tho tci tco
.B }
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.B effectiveness
.RB ( parallel | counter )
.I ntu cr
.B }
.br
.B ${thermo
.I id
.B fin
.I h k thickness length
.B }
.br
.B thermo del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Thermo
makes
.IR thermo (2)
usable from
.IR sh (1).
Units are SI and absolute, and the sign convention is heat in and work
out positive, exactly as there.
.PP
A handle carries a gas, a current state, the last process and the last
cycle.
Each process moves the current state and records what it cost, so
.B work,
.B heat,
.B du
and
.B ds
describe the process just run and
.B p,
.B v,
.B t
and the rest describe where it ended up.
.SS Cycles
.BR Carnot ,
.BR otto ,
.B diesel
and
.B brayton
each run a cycle;
.B efficiency,
.B wnet,
.B qin
and
.B qout
report it, and
.B states
gives the corners as
.I p v T
for each.
.PP
.B Path
is the one thing here that is not a thin wrapper.
It returns the loop as
.I v p
pairs,
.I n
a side, interpolated along the
.B actual
.B process
joining each pair of corners \- an isentrope where the cycle is
isentropic, an isotherm where it is isothermal, a straight line only
where the process really is one.
.PP
.B Path 1
is exactly the corners, which is what a plot of the state points wants.
.PP
That matters because the
.B area
inside a P-v loop is the work.
Joining the corners with straight lines draws the wrong area: an
isentrope bows well below its chord, and for a compression ratio of 9
the midpoint of one leg sits at 279 kPa where the chord says 1.29 MPa.
A diagram drawn the easy way overstates the work by eye.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
load std
load thermo
t := ${thermo new}
thermo $t gas air
thermo $t state pt 100000 300
thermo $t isentropic 1000000
echo ${thermo $t t}		# 579.2 K
echo ${thermo $t heat}		# 0, it is isentropic

thermo $t otto 9 300 100000 1e6
echo ${thermo $t efficiency}	# 0.58476 = 1 - r^(1-gamma)
echo ${thermo $t lmtd counter 400 350 300 350}	# 50
thermo del $t
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/thermo.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR thermo (2),
.IR thermotest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A gamma outside the range any gas has, a negative absolute temperature
or pressure, a cold reservoir hotter than the hot one, streams that
cross in an exchanger: each fails the command with the message
.IR thermo (2)
gives.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd .
.IR Thermo (2)
underneath it is checked by
.IR thermotest (1);
what is added here is the handle state and
.BR path ,
exercised by the example above and by
.BR /lib/demo/cyclelab.sh .
.PP
.B Path
knows the shape of the four cycles this module can run and nothing else.
A cycle assembled by hand out of individual processes has no path,
because the handle records only where each process ended and not which
process it was.
.PP
.B Van
.B der
.B Waals
is not reachable from here, so the one thing an ideal gas cannot do \-
have a critical point \- needs a Limbo program.
